<BaseLayout title="Conveyor Motors &#x26; Drive Systems Guide | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Guide to conveyor motors and drives: AC induction, gear motors, servo motors, VFDs, MDR motors, drum motors, motor sizing methodology, and IE3/IE4 energy efficiency standards." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Conveyor Motors &amp; Drives</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor motors convert electrical power into the mechanical torque and speed that drive belts, rollers, and chains. The most common conveyor motor is the AC induction motor paired with a gear reducer (gear motor), controlled by a VFD (variable frequency drive) for adjustable speed. Servo motors provide precise position control for indexing conveyors. Motorized drive rollers (MDR) embed a brushless DC motor inside a conveyor roller for zone-based accumulation. This guide covers the motor and drive types used in packaging conveyor systems, motor sizing, and the efficiency classes (IE3, IE4) that federal standards reference.</p>
<h2 id="ac-heading">AC Induction Motors</h2>
<p>AC induction motors are the workhorse of the conveyor industry. They run on standard 208V, 230V, or 460V three-phase power (or 115V/230V single-phase for fractional HP motors). The motor has no brushes, commutator, or permanent magnets, the rotor turns by electromagnetic induction from the stator winding. This simple construction makes AC induction motors reliable, inexpensive, and low-maintenance.</p>
<p>A 4-pole motor on 60 Hz power has a synchronous speed of 1,800 RPM (120 × 60 Hz / 4 poles), and runs slightly slower under load because of slip. That is far too fast for a conveyor pulley. A gear reducer between motor and pulley reduces speed and multiplies torque. The ratio is chosen from the belt speed and pulley diameter, as shown in the gear ratio section below.</p>
<h3>Gear Motors</h3>
<p>A gear motor integrates the AC motor and gear reducer into a single unit. This eliminates the coupling, mounting bracket, and alignment issues of separate motor and gearbox combinations. Gear motors are the standard drive for conveyor systems. Three gear reducer types dominate conveyor applications:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Helical gear reducers</strong>, high efficiency, smooth operation, low noise. Available in inline (shaft-in, shaft-out) and right-angle configurations. Helical gear motors are available from all major power transmission suppliers.</li>
<li><strong>Worm gear reducers</strong>, compact, lower cost, right-angle output as standard. Lower efficiency than helical gears, and efficiency falls as the ratio rises. Some high-ratio worm sets resist backdriving, but do not rely on that as a holdback on <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyors</a>: ask the supplier for a rated brake or backstop. Worm gear motors are widely available from multiple suppliers.</li>
<li><strong>Planetary gear reducers</strong>, highest torque density (smallest size for a given torque rating), high efficiency, inline configuration. Higher cost than helical or worm types. Used where space is constrained or high torque is required in a compact package.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Motor Enclosures</h3>
<p>Motor enclosure type matches the operating environment. TEFC (Totally Enclosed Fan Cooled) is the standard for dry industrial environments. TENV (Totally Enclosed Non-Ventilated) eliminates the external fan for applications where fan debris or noise is a concern. Washdown duty motors use stainless steel or coated housings, sealed bearings, and high IP ratings (such as IP69K) for high-pressure washdown. Explosion-proof motors (Class I/II, Division 1/2) are required in environments with flammable dust or vapors. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/components/">components guide</a> covers enclosure and frame material selection in more detail.</p>
<h2 id="vfd-heading">Variable Frequency Drives</h2>
<p>A VFD (<a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">variable frequency drive</a>) controls AC motor speed by converting incoming fixed-frequency AC power to variable-frequency, variable-voltage output. Synchronous speed is proportional to frequency: on a 60 Hz motor, 30 Hz gives about half speed. Running above 60 Hz is possible within the motor and gearbox ratings, but available torque falls above base speed. VFDs are the standard method for adjustable-speed conveyor operation.</p>
<h3>VFD Benefits for Conveyors</h3>
<p>VFDs provide soft starting, the motor accelerates gradually instead of starting at full speed. Soft starting reduces mechanical shock on belts, couplings, and gearboxes, and avoids the high inrush current of across-the-line starting. VFDs allow speed matching between conveyors and packaging machines, the PLC sends a speed reference to the VFD, and the VFD adjusts motor speed in real time. Dynamic braking through the VFD provides controlled deceleration without mechanical brakes.</p>
<p>Do not apply the fan and pump cube law to conveyors. The <a href="https://www.energy.gov/sites/prod/files/2014/04/f15/motor_tip_sheet11.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Department of Energy's ASD part-load tip sheet</a> explains that power varies with the cube of speed only for centrifugal fans and pumps. Conveyors are constant torque loads, where input power varies linearly with speed. Running a conveyor at half speed roughly halves its power, not cuts it to an eighth. The larger savings on conveyors usually come from stopping sections that have no product, not from slowing them.</p>
<h3>VFD Selection</h3>
<p>Size the VFD for the motor's full-load amperage (FLA) and the overload the application needs. Drive makers publish separate normal-duty and heavy-duty ratings. The heavy-duty rating allows a larger short-term overload and is the usual choice for conveyors with frequent loaded starts or high-inertia loads. Compare the drive's published overload rating with the starting torque the conveyor needs. When selecting a VFD, ensure it supports the required communication protocol for PLC integration, Ethernet/IP, PROFINET, or EtherCAT, and that it matches the conveyor motor's full-load amperage with appropriate overload margin.</p>
<h3>Decentralized VFDs</h3>
<p>Decentralized (motor-mounted) VFDs mount directly on the motor or gear motor, eliminating the long motor cable run from a central control panel. This reduces cable cost, reduces electrical interference, and simplifies installation. Decentralized VFDs are offered by most major drive suppliers as an alternative to panel-mounted drives. Decentralized drives suit conveyor systems with many individual drives spread over a large area, common in distribution and fulfillment centers.</p>
<h2 id="servo-heading">Servo Motors</h2>
<p>Servo motors provide precise speed, position, and torque control through closed-loop feedback. A high-resolution encoder on the motor shaft reports position to the servo drive, which adjusts motor current thousands of times per second to maintain the commanded position. Servo motors accelerate and decelerate faster than induction motors, making them the choice for indexing conveyors that must start, stop, and position product at exact locations.</p>
<p>Packaging applications for servo-driven conveyors include: indexing product under filling heads, positioning cartons for labeling or printing, synchronizing conveyor speed to wrapper or shrink tunnel speed via electronic gearing, and registering products to a fixed pitch for robotic pick-and-place. Servo systems are available from all major automation platform vendors, and selecting a servo system that matches the existing PLC platform simplifies integration and programming.</p>
<p>Servo systems cost significantly more than VFD-driven induction motors, the servo motor, servo drive, feedback cables, and programming time all add to the investment. Use servo drives where the application needs tight positioning, rapid acceleration and deceleration, or electronic synchronization with other axes. For simple variable-speed transport, a VFD-driven induction motor is more cost-effective. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a> covers motor sizing formulas applicable to both induction and servo motor systems.</p>
<h2 id="mdr-heading">Motorized Drive Rollers</h2>
<p>A motorized drive roller (MDR) is a conveyor roller with a brushless DC motor and planetary gear reducer built inside the roller tube. The MDR replaces an external motor, gearbox, belt, and chain. For example, the <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/rollerdrive/ec5000-straight-0c-to-40c" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll RollerDrive EC5000</a> is built in a 50 mm tube, runs on 24 V or 48 V DC, and comes in 20 W, 35 W, and 50 W versions. Zone controller cards manage the accumulation logic.</p>
<p>Each conveyor zone has one MDR that drives the adjacent idle rollers through O-ring or poly-V belts. Zone length is set by product length. A photoeye sensor at each zone detects product presence. When a zone is occupied and the downstream zone is also occupied, the zone controller stops the MDR, holding the product in place without contact pressure. When the downstream zone clears, the MDR restarts to advance the product. This provides zero-pressure accumulation without a central PLC. For more on MDR conveyor systems, see the <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors page</a>.</p>
<p>MDR systems save energy because zones run only when they have product to move, while a line-shaft or belt-driven roller conveyor runs its whole length continuously. They are also quiet. Interroll's EC5000 datasheet lists a maximum installed noise emission of 55 dB(A), application-dependent.</p>
<p>Torque depends on motor power and gear ratio. Interroll's <a href="https://www.interroll.com/fileadmin/products/product_data/EC-5000-IP54/EC5000_50mm_IP54_EN.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EC5000 IP54 datasheet</a> lists rated torques from 0.25 Nm (20 W, 9:1) to more than 5 Nm (50 W, high ratios), with lower top speed at higher ratios. Pick the ratio from the zone load and the speed you need, and check load per zone with the conveyor maker.</p>
<h2 id="drum-heading">Drum Motors</h2>
<p>A drum motor integrates the electric motor and gear reducer inside the drive pulley (drum) of a belt conveyor. The motor, gearbox, and bearings are sealed inside a stainless steel or aluminum shell filled with oil for lubrication and cooling. No external motor, gearbox, guards, or chain are required, the drum motor is the drive pulley.</p>
<p>Drum motors are ideal for sanitary and washdown environments because there are no external moving parts to guard or protect from water. The sealed construction eliminates nip points at the drive, prevents contamination from external lubricants, and is offered with high IP ratings for high-pressure washdown. Drum motors suit food-grade belt conveyors, inspection conveyors, and compact conveyors where space for an external motor is not available.</p>
<p>Drum motors come in a wide range of diameters and power ratings. Small-diameter drum motors allow tight end radii for close-gap product transfers. Check the maker's catalog for diameter, power, and speed combinations.</p>
<p>One limitation of drum motors: the motor is not field-serviceable. When a drum motor fails, the entire unit is replaced. Keep a spare drum motor on hand, and ask the supplier for rebuild turnaround time before you buy. For detailed parts and spares planning, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">conveyor parts guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="dc-heading">DC Motors</h2>
<p>DC motors were once the standard for variable-speed conveyor drives. DC motor speed is controlled by varying the armature voltage, simpler electronics than VFDs but requiring more motor maintenance (brushes and commutator wear). Brushed DC motors are now largely replaced by AC motors with VFDs for main conveyor drives.</p>
<p>Brushless DC (BLDC) motors remain widely used in MDR rollers and small conveyors. BLDC motors have no brushes or commutator, electronic commutation provides long life, high efficiency, and low maintenance. 24V BLDC motors power MDR conveyor zones, small tabletop conveyors, and conveyor accessories (pop-up transfers, pusher diverters). The low voltage (24V DC) simplifies wiring and improves safety compared to 480V AC systems.</p>
<p>90V DC gear motors are still found on older conveyor systems and on new conveyors where simple, low-cost speed control is needed. A DC motor controller (SCR drive) provides speed adjustment from a potentiometer or analog signal. 90V DC gear motors are still available from several motor suppliers. When replacing an aging DC drive system, VFD-driven AC motors are the recommended modern equivalent, they provide the same speed adjustability with lower maintenance. For maintenance considerations when upgrading drive systems, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">conveyor maintenance guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="efficiency-heading">Energy Efficiency</h2>
<p>Motor efficiency determines how much electrical energy is converted to mechanical output versus heat loss. Higher-efficiency motors reduce energy costs and run cooler, extending insulation and bearing life. International standards define motor efficiency classes to enable comparison across manufacturers.</p>
<h3>IEC Efficiency Classes</h3>
<p>IEC 60034-30-1:2014 defines efficiency classes IE1 (Standard), IE2 (High), IE3 (Premium), and IE4 (Super Premium). The U.S. Department of Energy's <a href="https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2023-05/em-ecs-dfr.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2023 electric motors direct final rule</a> notes that each IE step is roughly a 20 percent reduction in losses, and treats NEMA Premium as the IE3 level. Current DOE standards apply to motors manufactured on and after June 1, 2016. The 2023 rule raises motors from 100 to 250 hp to IE4 and keeps smaller motors at IE3. Most conveyor gearmotors are far below 100 hp, so check the rule that covers your motor's rating and type.</p>
<h3>Motor Sizing and Efficiency</h3>
<p>Motor efficiency drops at light load, which DOE's tip sheet also notes. An oversized motor running lightly loaded wastes energy and runs at a lower power factor. Proper motor sizing, using the belt pull and HP calculations from the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a>, ensures the motor operates near its rated load where efficiency is highest.</p>
<h3>VFD Impact on Efficiency</h3>
<p>A VFD has its own losses. DOE's part-load table lists PWM drive efficiencies of 95 to 97 percent at full rated output for 5 to 30 hp drives, falling at light load. For a conveyor that runs at full speed all day, a VFD adds soft starting and speed control but no steady-state energy savings.</p>
<h3>Synchronous Reluctance and PM Motors</h3>
<p>Synchronous reluctance (SynRM) and permanent magnet (PM) motors reach efficiency levels above IE3 induction motors, and several drive suppliers market them as IE4 or IE5 packages. <a href="https://global.abb/group/en/technology/did-you-know/ie5-synrm-motors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ABB</a>, for example, describes its SynRM motors as meeting the IE5 ultra-premium class defined by the IEC. Most require a matched VFD and cannot run across the line. Whether the extra cost pays back depends on run hours and energy price, so ask the supplier for efficiency at your actual load and speed.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Motor Sizing Methodology</h2>
<p>Motor sizing for conveyors follows five steps: calculate belt pull, calculate horsepower at the pulley, divide by drive efficiency, apply a service factor, and select the next standard motor size. The complete formulas and worked examples are in the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a>. This section summarizes the key points specific to motor selection.</p>
<h3>HP Formula</h3>
<p class="formula">HP = (Te × V) / 33,000</p>
<p>Where Te = effective tension (belt pull) in pounds and V = belt speed in feet per minute. This is the steady-state power at the drive pulley. Divide by drive train efficiency, apply the supplier's service factor or design margin, and round up to the next standard motor size. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a> works a full example using published friction coefficients.</p>
<h3>Gear Ratio Selection</h3>
<p>The gear ratio determines the output RPM from the motor RPM. Target the output RPM to produce the desired belt speed on the specified drive pulley diameter. Use the belt speed formula: V = π × D × RPM / 12. Solve for RPM: RPM = (V × 12) / (π × D). Then calculate the gear ratio: GR = Motor RPM / Required Output RPM. Select the nearest available gear ratio from the manufacturer's catalog. VFDs compensate for gear ratio mismatch by adjusting motor speed.</p>
<h3>Torque Verification</h3>
<p>After selecting the motor and gear reducer, verify that the output torque exceeds the required torque. Required torque (in-lbs) = Te × D / 2, where Te is belt pull in pounds and D is drive pulley diameter in inches. The gear motor's rated output torque must exceed this value with the supplier's service factor applied. Check the gear reducer's thermal rating as well, continuous operation at or near maximum torque can overheat the gear oil.</p>
