<h2 id="faq-heading">Motors &amp; Drives FAQ</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What type of motor is used on a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most packaging conveyors use AC induction gear motors, an AC motor integrated with a gear reducer. For variable speed, a VFD controls motor speed electronically. Servo motors handle precise indexing and positioning. MDR (motorized drive roller) motors use low-voltage brushless DC motors, commonly 24V, inside conveyor rollers for zone-based accumulation. Drum motors integrate the motor and gearbox inside the drive pulley for washdown belt conveyors.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a VFD and why is it used on conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A VFD (variable frequency drive) controls AC motor speed by varying the output frequency and voltage. VFDs enable adjustable conveyor speed, soft starting, and speed matching between conveyors and machines. Because conveyors are constant torque loads, power falls roughly in proportion to speed, not with the cube of speed. Select a VFD that supports the required PLC communication protocol and matches the motor's full-load amperage with appropriate overload capacity.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is an MDR motor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An MDR motor is a brushless DC motor and planetary gear reducer built inside a conveyor roller. Each zone has one MDR driving adjacent idle rollers. MDR systems provide zero-pressure accumulation, low noise, and energy efficiency because only active zones are powered. When specifying MDR rollers, compare torque ratings, speed ranges, and zone controller compatibility across available suppliers.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you size a motor for a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Calculate belt pull from conveyor length, load, friction, and elevation. Apply HP = (belt pull × belt speed) / 33,000. Divide by drive efficiency, apply the supplier's service factor, and round up to the next standard motor size. Verify output torque exceeds required torque. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a> for detailed formulas and examples.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id="specify-the-right-drive-system">Specify the Right Drive System</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a> for motor sizing formulas, or return to the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">design guide hub</a> for the complete conveyor system design process.</p>
